well the man overseeing our future  relationship with the eu the chancellor  of the dutch of lancaster cabinet office  minister michael goff  joins us in the studio good to see you  this morning good morning  we should say this is a moment of  significance isn't it an agreement in  principle  on that rather phony issue of the irish  border yes absolutely  uh throughout this year there have been  intense talks between  ourselves and the european commission in  order to make sure that we can resolve  the issues  on what's called the northern ireland  protocol and of course people wanted to  make sure  first of all that there was no border  infrastructure on northern ireland  island border but also that northern  ireland can be a secure part of the  united kingdom  we've agreed that uh as a result some of  the uh the measures that we were putting  forward which  some in europe had criticized we can now  no longer  we no longer need to introduce those um  and that means that uh  there is a smoother glide path towards a  possible deal  it could be viewed as a concession being  made on the british part  given at the threat that we had made to  break international law so what have we  gained from making this concession  well unless we'd secured agreement um on  the northern ireland protocol  then we faced a situation where goods  going to northern ireland would have  faced  tariffs and the price of everything from  a pint of milk to a nissan qashqai  would have been greater for citizens in  northern ireland uh it's also the case  that we might have faced restrictions on  some of the food that would have been  found on supermarket shelves  it would also have been the case that  there might have been barriers that  people would have  would have tried to erect between  northern ireland businesses  and great britain none of those threats  now exist and as a result  of the uh determined way um in which the  prime minister has negotiated  are we're in a stronger position to be  able to say to the people of northern  ireland that they are secure in the  united kingdom  but also of course all the gains of the  peace process over the last 22 years  are secured as well it does though very  much look like  a border down the irish sea there will  be checks  minimal checks perhaps but there will be  checks between great britain  and northern ireland and wasn't it boris  johnson you said that you know over my  dead body  there would be a border in the irish sea  i don't think there's a border in the  irish sea  what are the checks taking place there  it's always been the case that uh  the whole island of ireland has been  regarded as a single zone when it comes  to  to animal health and it's always been  the case that there's been  uh checks on live animals when they've  gone from greek britain  to northern ireland uh there are minimal  additional number of checks  on products of animal origin and we put  this forward  um during the talks last year  as a way of breaking the deadlock and  making sure that we could avoid  any sort of border infrastructure on the  island of ireland as well  but the the impact for businesses and  for citizens in northern ireland  will be minimal citizens are going to be  able to enjoy all the products that they  currently enjoy  and the security of knowing that their  position within the united kingdom is  absolutely stable the  the checks may well be minimal and we  will discover this as as we move forward  but  but they remain checks and they remain  border checks and there is a difference  it would be it has to be said now  between great britain  and northern ireland in terms of its  long-lasting relationship with the  european union i mean let's not forget  we're also going to have not eu  officials stationed in northern ireland  now  by my reading and perpetuity well there  are two things to say  the first thing is that that northern  ireland's position is different from the  rest of the united kingdom and one  particular way  which is that it has a land border with  the european union um and as a result of  that  there are certain specific additional  measures that we need to put in place  and also it has been the case that  northern ireland's position ever since  the signing of the belfast  agreement um has been uh unique in the  united kingdom  but it's firmly part of the uk and the  principle of consent underlies this  as for eu officials it's always the case  when you have trade arrangements  that people from another country with  whom you've got a trading relationship  can come can inspect and can make sure  that your facilities  meet their standards and vice versa why  then  is it that unionists in northern ireland  who are arguably best placed  to decide whether or not there is a  border down the irc  are less than sanguine about a long-term  eu presence in northern ireland  and indeed about the potential of there  being different regulatory regimes  there and here well i think when we  started this whole process  uh we wanted to make sure that we uh uh  respected the uh position of both  traditions in northern ireland  um now i'm a unionist myself i want to  make sure that uh uh northern ireland  stays part of the united kingdom  and i'll argue for the benefits of that  but it's also the case that over time  we've acknowledged  that there are some specific provisions  that exist in northern ireland  that that take account of the fact that  it has a border with uh the republic  and with the european union but if we  were to draw a venn diagram of the uk  and the european union northern ireland  would in essence be the bit where the  the two intersect right now you said to  my colleague sam quotes yesterday look  this provides northern ireland with with  the best of both worlds  here he asked the same question that i'm  going to ask right now why not the best  of both worlds for  scotland england wales if it's good  enough for northern ireland  why not the rest of us well as you point  out there are some people in northern  ireland who  are critical of some some aspects of  what we've achieved and feel that uh  there are downsides to this it is a  balanced package  overall it provides businesses in  northern ireland  with access to the single market because  again there's no border infrastructure  on the island of ireland as you pointed  out there are some additional  administrative checks uh on some goods  going  from britain to northern ireland not the  other way around um and  that that balanced package means that uh  businesses in northern ireland have  access both to the rest of the uk  and to the eu but ultimately northern  ireland's position is unique  its history its geography its politics  mean that it's in a different position  uh to the rest of the united kingdom  even as it remains  solidly part of one country what does  all this this mean for the prospect of a  trade deal certainly the mood music from  both sides  doesn't exactly lend much confidence to  the fact that we're going to get any  progress in the immediate future  but when given that what assistance is  the government preparing  for for businesses that will have a  negative impact because of a no deal  brexit  well firstly i hope that we will secure  a free trade agreement but secondly  in the event that we don't have a  negotiated agreement there are a number  of steps that the chancellor and others  can take in order to make sure  that british business is as competitive  as possible  and over the course of this year we've  been investing significant amounts in  order to help business to prepare  can we talk about vaccines because of  course yesterday was it was an  incredibly significant day and indeed  now we heard the news yesterday as well  of the  oxford vaccine ready to come on stream  now i mean first of all your  your thoughts on the fact that we are  now starting to see that roll out and  when we might actually see  other vaccines being brought on stream  well uh yesterday was a great day  um uh as we know the world was watching  as the very first vaccinations that  occurred  occurred here in the uk thanks to the  nhs  um we know that more vaccine is arriving  from the  uh uh the the the current level of  stocks which are coming from belgium  but we also know that uh the oxford  astrozenica virus  uh passed some more tests yesterday um  and i hope but again  i have to defer to the official medical  regulator uh that will be able to be  injecting people with that vaccine  soon what does this mean for the easing  of restrictions matt hancock appeared to  suggest  yesterday that when the most vulnerable  in society have received the vaccine  that is the point at which things would  start getting back to normal i mean can  you put a time scale on that  no i don't think i can at this stage i  think the most important thing is to  make sure exactly as matt outlines  that we vaccinate the most vulnerable  then we want to make sure that  vaccination spreads across the whole  population  um i think that we can begin  progressively to ease measures in the  new year  but certainly we need during january and  february which tough months for the nhs  not to relax our vigilance what what  what exactly are we expecting to happen  to the nhs  come january everyone knows you ease the  restrictions  covered transmission increases that will  happen over christmas it's a certainty  do we have any estimates of the  additional burden that's going to be  placed on the nhs  well the nhs always keeps the the level  of pressure that it's under  under review um and it's you know you're  absolutely right  uh people are going to be allowed to mix  um over christmas  uh it's a natural human desire to be  with those that we love at that time of  the year  and that's one of the reasons why we  have the tier system in place  some people have understandably uh found  the current tier system  very difficult and it is but it's there  to try to ensure  that we uh keep the pressure on the nhs  down through those difficult months  so so where then would we see the  restrictions being eased in the new year  which particular parts can we say look  that is going to be the first thing  well again this is something that we we  have to keep under review  um we know that the virus is a a  resilient and  difficult enemy we know that concerted  action in areas such as liverpool  have brought the instance down and we've  been able to ease restrictions  but we just have to keep this constantly  under review no one more than me  would like to see more and more of the  country move into lower tiers  but of course as you quite rightly point  out we need to make sure the nhs is  protected through  the next two difficult months i mean is  london about to enter  into tier three by your reckoning  certainly there are people in other  parts of the country with higher  infection rates  than there are sorry lower infection  rates that there are in part of london  pointing the finger at london and  suggesting that it's been singled out  for special treatment  no i don't think that's true i mean i  think you're right that there are  variations across london borough by  borough  uh but we get advice from the joint  biosecurity center  um and from those who are monitoring not  just the incidence of the virus but also  the pressure on the nhs  and we do keep these things under review  but of course what we want to do is to  see areas  if we possibly can either stay in their  current tier  or move down a tier but it requires us  all particularly in the right christmas  to be vigilant  it seems what you're saying there that  london might not be about to enter into  tier three there well it's not  my personal judgment it's it's you know  it's it's not the case that any  individual minister  or indeed any individual advisor decides  we look at the evidence and we form a  collective judgment and of course we  share that evidence so people can see  the basis  on which the decision was made a little  later in the program i'm going to be  speaking to fred banning and he has been  diagnosed with category four at bowel  cancer  terminal a horrible thing he is asking  for people in similar situations to him  to be prioritized for vaccinations so  that in the remaining weeks months of  his life  you can spend time with the family but  what do you make of that um well  it's an incredibly um  you know moving situation when you have  people who are living with terminal  diseases and we're  uh reviewing how we can make sure that  the most vulnerable  and those in in clinically difficult  positions can  be vaccinated and we were discussing  exactly how we can do this  in government only yesterday obviously  it depends on the number of vaccines  that we can secure  but absolutely when you have a moving  example like that and  there are all too many we want to make  sure that people are  vaccinated so they can be um with those  they love  uh at what is inevitably um an  incredibly difficult and stressful time  for  for for the family uh well i know that  fred will be keeping his fingers crossed  that there may be some movement in that  regard sometime soon  michael golf always nice to see you  thanks for joining us no no thank you  thank you

